            <ArticleTitle>Real-world Outcomes of Hepatitis C Treatment during the Interferon-free Era at an Urban Safety-net Hospital.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1333-1344</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1353/hpu.2017.0118</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Little is known about real-world outcomes for new interferon-free treatment for hepatitis C virus (HCV) among underserved and diverse communities.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify predictors of treatment prescription and evaluate outcomes as measured by sustained virologic response (SVR) with HCV RNA testing three months after therapy completion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective cohort at a safety-net health care system.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients with (1) at least one clinical visit between December 6, 2013, and December 31st 2014; and (2) at least three months follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="KEY RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Predominantly non-White cohort (61%). Of 1,284 HCV-infected patients 121 prescribed sofosbuvir-based therapy. Severe liver fibrosis (OR 1.66, 95% CI 1.05, 2.64) independently associated with treatment prescription. In those with evaluable HCV RNA, SVR was 99%.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Cure rates similar to clinical trial data can be achieved in diverse underserved communities.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
